% =======================================================
\section{Initialization and Reference Alignment}
\label{sec:init_alignment}
\label{sec:startup-policy}

Startup keeps attitude initialization outside the MEKF.  The measurement-only
Mahony observer of Sec.~\ref{sec:vertical-proxy} runs from the first sample
while the MEKF remains untouched; wave-period and acceleration-variance
estimators converge in parallel.  Once tilt, tuner state, and, for full-heading
operation, an adopted magnetic/equivalent heading gauge are adequate, the proxy
solution initializes the MEKF in Live mode.  The resulting state is not
required to lie immediately inside the final regional invariant set.
Sec.~\ref{sec:finite-live-capture} instead certifies a larger structured
post-handoff set from which the complete nonlinear full-heading Live recursion
can reach that invariant set in finite time.  Gravity-only operation has a yaw
symmetry and is treated separately by Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-nomag-quotient}.

\subsection{Tilt and operating-point bootstrap}

Before magnetic north is established, yaw is a gauge degree of freedom.  The
proxy initializes roll and pitch from accelerometer direction and evolves by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:proxy-mahony}.  Its low correction bandwidth avoids interpreting
ordinary orbital acceleration as rapid tilt, while the integral term estimates
standing gyro-bias tilt.

In parallel, the adaptation front end stages the estimated
$(\tau,\sigma_{aw},r_S)$ operating point.  These are model parameters rather
than state corrections, so the first propagated MEKF sample uses the best
available sea-state schedule.

\subsection{Quality-gated handoff}
\label{sec:proxy-handoff}

Normal handoff requires: (i) \SI{2}{s} of proxy gravity agreement with
sine-angle error below \num{0.075} (about \SI{4.3}{\degree}) on the aligned
branch---predicted and measured gravity directions with positive inner
product---and gyro magnitude below \SI{30}{\degree\per\second}; (ii) a
magnetic gauge when a magnetometer is present and full-heading operation is
requested; and (iii) tuner readiness.  A minimum \SI{8}{s} startup prevents a
quiet initial interval from passing prematurely.  A \SI{150}{s} timeout bounds
startup duration without interrupting active magnetic-reference acquisition.

At handoff, the proxy attitude seeds the MEKF once.  With magnetic north
established, attitude covariance uses
\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{tilt}}=\SI{0.035}{rad},\qquad
\sigma_{\mathrm{yaw}}=\SI{0.087}{rad};
\]
without a magnetic gauge, $\sigma_{\mathrm{yaw}}=\pi/2$.  The linear
$[v,p,S,a_w]$ block, current OU parameters, and integral regularizer are enabled
together.  Accelerometer-bias learning remains gated until the magnetic
refinement logic releases it because bias and small tilt error are weakly
separable in waves and the residual bias correlation time is long.

The gravity residual is a sine of the alignment angle, so it does not by itself
distinguish aligned and antipodal branches.  The gate therefore also tests the
sign of the inner product, and every accepted handoff satisfies
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-aligned-branch}.  The \SI{150}{s} timeout is held to
that same branch test, so a forced handoff is delayed rather than accepted on
the antipodal branch.

The stability handoff separates tilt accuracy from heading-reference accuracy.
The normal gravity gate gives the transverse attitude bound
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-tilt}.  When a magnetic/equivalent heading
reference is accepted, it defines the \emph{adopted horizontal gauge} used by
the full-heading Live proof.  The internal heading error relative to that gauge
is bounded by Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-yaw-internal}.  A residual
magnetic reference offset $b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}$ contributes to true-north
heading error through Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-true-yaw-error}, while the complete
horizontal world-frame trajectory is represented in the jointly rotated gauge
of Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-gauge-world-vectors}.  Thus a fixed standing compass
reference offset is not mistaken for estimator instability.

The accepted tilt/internal-heading bounds and independent finite physical
bounds on the remaining MEKF errors define the structured handoff set
$\mathcal H$ of Sec.~\ref{sec:semiglobal-handoff}.  In the full-heading
sensitive/kinematic certificate metrics, the normal startup interface is
\begin{equation}
 \vct R_H^{\max}\prec\vct R_C.
\end{equation}
The handoff need not satisfy the block-local invariant-tube condition of
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local} on its first Live measurement.

Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture} then propagates the complete implemented
nonlinear full-heading map, including covariance staging and the full $S=0$
gain, through exact comparison envelopes.  Individual early lifts may expand as
long as their prefixes remain in the chart-safe proof domain.  The handoff
sequence reaches the inner regional set after the finite count $N_H$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-count}, after which the same regional theorem supplies
practical ISS directly.

A timeout-forced handoff does not test the 0.075 residual magnitude and
therefore does not inherit the normal tilt bound.  It belongs to the composite
stability result only when an independent timeout-state envelope yields
$\vct R_{H,T}^{\max}\prec\vct R_C$; Phase~C derives that envelope from an
explicit Mahony Lyapunov estimate.

\subsection{Two-stage magnetic reference}
\label{sec:mag-two-stage}

Magnetic acquisition uses proxy tilt rather than the MEKF.  Raw magnetometer
samples are averaged in a yaw-stripped level frame, preventing arbitrary proxy
yaw from contaminating field direction; the time-based window spans multiple
wave periods to reduce wave-correlated levelling error.

Two stages balance availability and reference accuracy.  A provisional
reference is accepted after the gravity gate, typically enabling heading and
MEKF handoff within \SIrange{22}{52}{s} in the scored simulations.  A second
\SI{30}{s} acquisition beginning at \SI{90}{s} relearns the reference after the
low-bandwidth proxy settles.  It remains proxy framed to avoid self-confirmation
from the already-live MEKF.

Acceptance of the refined reference replaces the world magnetic reference,
re-gauges yaw once when the independent acquisition supplies a finite yaw, and
releases the accelerometer-bias hold.  The source leaves tilt and all additive
navigation variables numerically unchanged and does not replace the covariance;
only the bias-enable path may apply its bounded covariance floor.
Section~\ref{sec:hybrid-mag-jump} therefore models this event as the explicit
jump $\mathcal R_M$, not as an ordinary Kalman sample.  Because the proof's
heading gauge is a complete horizontal-frame rotation, changing that gauge also
changes the reference values of $(v,p,S,a_w)$ even though their stored source
variables are untouched.  Equations~\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-world-error-jump}--
\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-world-error-bound} account for this coordinate-reference
jump using a bounded gauge change and bounded physical world-vector magnitudes.
The magnetic jump certificate separately bounds the internal heading residual
through Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-heading-residual}.  If the resulting exact jump
envelope satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}, the event returns directly
to the outer capture set and Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery} supplies
subsequent hybrid safety and recovery.

The continuous hard-iron correction that follows refinement is not another hard
attitude jump.  It slews the applied body-frame offset and the magnetic model
without writing attitude or covariance, so it belongs to the bounded normal-Live
mode family rather than restarting the hybrid capture argument at every update.

\subsection{Live re-lock boundary}

A sustained estimated tilt above $70^\circ$ for \SI{0.35}{s} can trigger a
Live tilt re-lock; a \SI{3}{s} cooldown prevents another reset while its timer is
positive.  The exact source map preserves the pre-reset Euler yaw, rebuilds
roll/pitch from the instantaneous accelerometer, reseeds the attitude covariance,
and zeros attitude cross-covariances while retaining the additive navigation and
bias states.  Section~\ref{sec:hybrid-tilt-jump} therefore treats it as a
separate jump $\mathcal R_T$.

A deterministic post-reset tilt guarantee requires the specific-force condition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-force-bound}; the $70^\circ$ trigger alone does not
impose that measurement-quality bound.  Because the implementation preserves an
Euler yaw coordinate, the proof also retains the nonsingular-yaw-chart condition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-yaw-chart}.  Under those conditions the reset obeys
the tilt bound Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-reset-bound} and preserves internal yaw
as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-yaw-preserved}.  If its exact post-jump envelope
satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}, the reset returns to
$\mathcal C_k(\vct R_C)$.

The cooldown gives the source-level no-Zeno bound
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-dwell}.  If only finitely many hard resets occur, the
last one is followed by finite regional capture and the ordinary practical-ISS
bound.  If re-locks continue indefinitely, Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery}
claims recurrent capture only on inter-reset intervals long enough to complete
the verified recovery sequence; repeated hard resets are not silently treated
as asymptotically vanishing disturbances.
